  presentations. In an age of rapidly‐evolving technology and uptake of smartphones and tablet devices, mHealth‐based   
  technology (defined by the World Health Organization as mobile and wireless technologies to achieve health            
  objectives) is an innovative way to provide health education which has the benefit of being able to reach people who  
  are unable or unwilling to access traditional heart failure education programmes and services.",                      
          "review_objective": "To systematically review and quantify the potential benefits and harms of                
  mHealth‐delivered education for people with heart failure.",                                                          
          "selection_criteria": "We included studies if they were conducted as a randomised controlled trial (RCT),     
  involving adults (≥ 18 years) with a diagnosis of HF. We included trials comparing mHealth‐delivered education such   
  as internet and web‐based education programmes for use on smartphones and tablets (including apps) and other mobile   
  devices, SMS messages and social media‐delivered education programmes, versus usual HF care."                         
