emphasizing technology-mediated self-management support rather than remote monitoring alone unless it incorporated clear
educational elements (e.g., symptom feedback prompting self-care decisions, coaching, or educational messages). Studies 
evaluating technology primarily for monitoring vital signs (weight, BP, ECGs) without explicit educational content were 
generally excluded unless they included interactive self-management components (e.g., symptom questionnaires, coaching).
This aligns with the review's objective to quantify benefits/harms of mHealth‐delivered education specifically. The     
conservative approach was particularly important for studies with limited methodological detail in their abstracts,     
where uncertainty about RCT design led to inclusion rather than exclusion.                                              
